ML_PAGE("l11-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l11\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 11 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Chapter 11 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 30 multiple-choice questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with the answer, the logic and the exam trap hidden until you reveal them.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>MCQs in the notes</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Exam traps explained</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Difficult-mode questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l11\" data-quiz=\"11\">Start Chapter 11 simulator</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l11\" id=\"l11p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">2 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</li><li data-full=\"Questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l11p-s2\" title=\"Questions\">30 practice questions</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - FORMULAS AND REVIEW</li><li data-full=\"Formula map and error diagnosis\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l11p-s4\" title=\"Formula map and error diagnosis\">Formula map and error diagnosis</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l11\" open><summary><div><strong>Chapter 11 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Exam strategy, practice questions, formula sheets and review - section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>2 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 11 - Bond Valuation: MCQ Practice</h2>\n<p><strong>30 questions · suggested time: 50 minutes · practice target: 80%.</strong> These are illustrative, not official exam requirements. Select one best answer and try the questions before the separate key. Assume level yield discounting, annual coupon payments and no taxes or transaction costs unless otherwise stated. Numerical answers are rounded.</p>\n<p>First identify the <strong>cash flows and their timing</strong>, then choose the discount rate per matching period. Finally interpret the outcome in terms of price, reinvestment and investor needs.</p><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l11p-d1\">PART A - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l11p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>An investor's simple required-return model includes a 2% real rate, 3% expected inflation premium and 2% risk premium. What is the approximate required nominal return under this additive model?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 7%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 6%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 12%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Additive required return = 2% + 3% + 2% = <strong>7%</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> This is the stated approximation; exact compounding can differ.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>A corporate bond yields 6% and a comparable-maturity government bond yields 4%. What is the yield spread?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 10 percentage points.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.02 basis points.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 2 percentage points, or 200 basis points.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Exactly the probability of corporate default.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Spread = 6% - 4% = <strong>2 percentage points = 200 basis points</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A yield spread reflects multiple potential factors and is not itself a default probability.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>Why might a callable bond offer a higher yield than an otherwise comparable noncallable bond?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Callable bonds cannot be redeemed before maturity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A call gives the investor a guaranteed higher realized return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Investors may require compensation because the issuer can redeem it early under the contract.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A call removes reinvestment risk.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>An issuer's call option can deprive holders of above-market coupons; investors may demand compensation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A higher quoted yield does not guarantee the bond remains outstanding to maturity.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>A yield curve plots comparable bonds' yields against:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Annual dividend payout ratios.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Company revenue in a future year.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The investor's original purchase price only.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Time to maturity at a particular date.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A yield curve compares yields by maturity at a point in time, ideally for comparable credit and instrument characteristics.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> It is not a graph of one bond's realized returns through time.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>A yield curve shows a 2-year yield of 3% and a 10-year yield of 5%. What is its slope between those points?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Not measurable because yields are percentages.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Inverted.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Necessarily flat.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Upward-sloping.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The 10-year yield is above the 2-year yield, so this portion slopes upward.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Curve shape is an observation, not a certain forecast.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>Under a simplified pure expectations hypothesis, an upward-sloping yield curve can reflect:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Proof that liquidity premiums cannot exist in real markets.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Certainty that actual future short rates will rise exactly as predicted.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Expectations of higher future short-term interest rates.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A guaranteed capital gain on all long-term bonds.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>In the simplified expectations view, an upward slope can indicate higher expected future short rates.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Actual future rates need not match expectations; other theories include premiums or segmentation.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>What does liquidity-preference theory add to an interpretation of a yield curve?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Maturity never affects bond yields.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Longer-maturity bonds must have no price risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Investors may demand additional compensation for holding longer-maturity bonds with greater risk or less liquidity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Every future rate can be observed with certainty.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Liquidity preference allows a term premium reflecting investors' preferences and risks.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A positive slope can arise without certainty that future short rates will increase.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>Market-segmentation theory emphasizes:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A single mandatory interest rate across all maturities.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Supply and demand within maturity segments shaped by borrower and investor preferences.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> That every investor holds only common stock.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Guaranteed success from predicting the next recession.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Segmentation emphasizes distinct maturity preferences and supply and demand.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A curve's shape has more than one possible interpretation.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>A fixed-rate bond's required yield rises, with its promised cash flows unchanged. What happens to its theoretical present price?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The coupon rate automatically rises to match the yield.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It rises.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It stays fixed at par in all circumstances.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It falls.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Higher discount rates reduce the present value of unchanged promised cash flows.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Fixed coupons remain fixed while their market value changes.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>A two-year bond has RM1,000 par, pays RM80 at each year-end and must yield 10% annually. What is its theoretical price today?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM1,160.00.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM1,000.00.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM1,034.71.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Approximately RM965.29.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Price = $80/1.10+1{,}080/1.10^2=72.73+892.56=\\text{RM}965.29$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Include principal with the final coupon and discount each at the correct date.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>A two-year RM1,000 par bond has an 8% annual coupon paid semiannually and a 10% nominal annual required yield compounded semiannually. Which calculation gives its price?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Discount RM40 four times at 10% per half-year.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Discount RM80 for two years at 10% per half-year.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Discount RM40 every half-year plus RM1,000 at the fourth half-year at 5% per half-year.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Discount RM1,000 only and omit all coupons.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Annual coupon is RM80, so each half-year payment is RM40; 10% nominal divided by two is 5% per period, with four periods.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Convert the coupon, yield and number of periods together.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>Using the assumptions of Q11, what is the approximate bond price?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM964.54.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM1,000.00.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM1,035.46.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM1,080.00.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$P=\\sum_{t=1}^{4}40/(1.05)^t+1{,}000/(1.05)^4\\approx\\text{RM}964.54$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> This is a discount bond because its coupon rate is below the required yield.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>For a standard fixed-rate bond, the coupon rate equals its market-required yield on a matching payment basis. Its theoretical price is generally:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Zero.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Above par.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Below par.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> At par.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>When coupon rate equals the required yield on the same payment basis, the present value of the standard bond equals par.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Check compounding and payment frequency before comparing rates.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>A bond with RM1,000 par pays RM80 annually and currently sells for RM900. Its current yield is closest to:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 8.00%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 8.89%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 9.00%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 11.11%.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Current yield = RM80 / RM900 = <strong>8.89%</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Coupon rate is 8%, and YTM also incorporates the redemption amount and timing.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>For an annual-pay noncallable bond trading below par, with positive coupon and no default, which ordering generally applies?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Coupon rate &gt; current yield &gt; YTM.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> YTM &gt; current yield &gt; coupon rate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Current yield &gt; YTM &gt; coupon rate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> All three are necessarily equal.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A discount makes annual coupon/current price exceed coupon/par, while convergence to par adds to YTM under the stated conditions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The ordering depends on assumptions and price relative to par.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>Yield to maturity (YTM) is best described as:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The annual coupon divided only by the current price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The discount rate equating the bond's current price with its promised coupon and maturity payments.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The guaranteed return over any chosen short holding period.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The coupon divided by par in every case.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>YTM is the internal rate of return implied by the current price and promised payments through maturity.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> It is not current yield and does not guarantee the investor's realized return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>An investor holds a fixed-rate bond to maturity and receives every promised payment. To realize its quoted YTM as a compound return over the full horizon, the standard calculation assumes interim coupons are:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Reinvested at the quoted YTM on the corresponding compounding basis.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Spent immediately while still earning the original bond's compound return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Reinvested at zero with no consequence.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Replaced by stock dividends.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The usual compound-return interpretation requires coupons to be reinvested at the quoted YTM and all payments to be received.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Holding to maturity alone does not remove reinvestment or default risk.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>When calculating yield to call for a bond callable in three years, which cash flows are used?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Coupons until final maturity and a guaranteed redemption at par in three years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Coupons through the assumed call date and the contractual call price at that date.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Only the current price and no future cash flows.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The call premium alone with no coupon payments.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Yield to call assumes the specified call occurs and uses coupons through that date plus the call price.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> It is conditional, not proof the issuer will call.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>A bond is purchased for RM900, pays RM60 after one year and is then sold ex-coupon for RM930. What is the one-year holding-period return before costs and taxes?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 3.33%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 10%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 6.67%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 13.33%.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>HPR = $(930-900+60)/900=90/900=\\mathbf{10\\%}$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Include both the sale-price change and the coupon exactly once.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>A bond trades below par. Which statement about its current yield is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It fully captures every reinvestment and call outcome.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It necessarily equals YTM.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It measures annual coupon income relative to price but does not by itself include the gain or loss on redemption.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It equals the fixed coupon rate regardless of price.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Current yield includes annual coupon income relative to current price, but omits any redemption gain or loss.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> It is not a complete measure of total return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>Macaulay duration is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The present-value-weighted average time to the bond's promised cash flows.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Always equal to the time remaining until final maturity for every coupon bond.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The annual coupon divided by market price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The issuer's credit rating expressed in years.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Macaulay duration weights each payment date by that payment's present value as a proportion of price.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Time to final maturity ignores the timing of intermediate coupons.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>A two-year zero-coupon bond has a single promised payment at maturity. What is its Macaulay duration, assuming a positive price?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Two years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Zero years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> One year.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It depends on coupon reinvestment.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>All promised cash arrives in year 2, so its PV weight is 100% and duration is <strong>2 years</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Yield affects price, but not the timing of the sole payment.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>A two-year RM1,000 par bond pays RM100 at year 1 and RM1,100 at year 2 and yields 10% annually. Its price is RM1,000. What is its Macaulay duration?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Approximately 1.91 years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1.00 year.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 2.10 years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 10.00 years.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$D=[1(100/1.1)+2(1{,}100/1.1^2)]/1{,}000\\approx\\mathbf{1.91}$ years.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Weight times by present value, not raw cash-flow amounts.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>Under annual compounding, a bond's Macaulay duration is 1.9091 years and yield is 10%. Its modified duration is approximately:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1.74 years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1.00 year.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1.91 years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 2.10 years.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Modified duration = Macaulay duration / $(1+y)$ = 1.9091 / 1.10 ≈ <strong>1.74</strong> under annual compounding.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use the yield per payment period when compounding frequency differs.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>A bond has modified duration 5.0. For a small 0.20 percentage-point yield increase, the duration-only approximation to price change is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> +1.0%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> -1.0%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> -10%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> -0.1%.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$\\Delta P/P\\approx-D_{mod}\\Delta y=-5(0.002)=-0.01=\\mathbf{-1.0\\%}$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A 0.20 percentage-point move equals 0.002 in decimal, not 0.20.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>Two otherwise comparable coupon bonds have equal maturities and yields, but Bond A pays a higher coupon. Which generally has the shorter Macaulay duration?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Bond A, because a greater share of its present-value cash flows arrives earlier.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The lower-coupon bond, in every comparable case.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both must have the same duration as maturity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Neither has duration because they pay coupons.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The higher coupon places more present-value weight on earlier cash flows, shortening duration in the stated comparison.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Duration and maturity are not generally equal.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>What is the principal intuition behind duration-based immunization of a fixed future liability?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Guarantee the issuer cannot default.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Ensure every possible future interest-rate and credit shock has no effect without rebalancing.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Eliminate the need to know the liability date.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Match asset duration to the investment horizon so that, under simplifying assumptions, price and reinvestment effects can offset.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Duration matching can balance first-order price and reinvestment effects for a specified horizon under assumptions such as a parallel rate change and rebalancing.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> It is not protection from every rate shape, credit or liquidity shock.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>An investor expects yields to rise and seeks to reduce immediate interest-rate sensitivity, all else equal. Which is more consistent with that objective?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Assume price cannot change before maturity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A longer-duration portfolio solely because maturity is later.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The greatest possible leverage regardless of risks.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A shorter-duration portfolio.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Lower modified duration generally means a smaller immediate percentage price response to a small rise in yields.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A rate forecast can be wrong, and other risks still matter.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>Which best describes a bond ladder?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Buy only equities with increasing dividends.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Hold one bond forever with no maturity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Hold bonds with staggered maturities and reinvest proceeds as bonds mature according to the strategy.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Guarantee the highest available future yield.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>A ladder staggers maturities and reinvests maturing proceeds, spreading reinvestment dates.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> It does not guarantee the highest future yield or eliminate credit risk.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>An investor considers selling one bond and buying a higher-yielding alternative. What is the strongest evaluation of a yield-pickup swap?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Compare credit, liquidity, call terms, maturity, taxes and transaction costs as well as the stated yield.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The higher yield is free extra return regardless of all other differences.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Credit and call provisions are irrelevant to yield.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A higher yield proves the new bond's price cannot fall.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A yield pickup can reflect different risks or costs; examine the complete terms and after-cost consequences.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A higher stated yield is not automatically a better risk-adjusted choice.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l11p-d3\">PART B - FORMULAS AND REVIEW</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l11p-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Formula map and error diagnosis</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l11_yield_order\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>The ordering of coupon rate, current yield and YTM depends only on whether the price is below, at or above par.</figcaption></figure></div><ul>\n<li><strong>Bond price</strong> = PV of every coupon + PV of maturity principal. For semiannual bonds divide the annual coupon and nominal annual yield by two and multiply years by two.</li>\n<li><strong>Current yield</strong> = annual coupon / current price. <strong>YTM</strong> discounts promised cash flows through maturity; <strong>YTC</strong> uses the assumed call date and call price. <strong>Holding-period return</strong> = (income + sale price - purchase price) / purchase price.</li>\n<li><strong>Macaulay duration</strong> = $\\sum t\\,PV(CF_t)/P$. Under annual compounding, <strong>modified duration</strong> = Macaulay duration / $(1+y)$; for small yield changes, $\\Delta P/P\\approx-D_{mod}\\Delta y$. Convexity and nonparallel shifts limit this approximation.</li>\n<li>Yield curves and strategies are interpretations with assumptions, not certain predictions. Investigate call terms, issuer quality, horizon, liquidity, costs and tax rules before selecting bonds.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Classify errors as cash-flow timing, annual versus period rates, price-yield direction, yield definition, duration units, or an unsupported guarantee. Reconstruct the timeline and identify which assumption could change the decision.</p>\n<p><em>Source note:</em> Original questions based on Chapter 11, <em>Bond Valuation</em> (Pearson slides, 2014). The slides' immunization and YTM descriptions require the stated reinvestment, interest-rate and credit assumptions; they do not promise realized returns under every possible outcome. Practice timing and targets are illustrative.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l11-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Difficult mode - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry practice, CFA-style, case competitions, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the chapter&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"11\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Chapter 11 difficult mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"11\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which description matches the slides' definition of a <strong>yield curve</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A graph of one bond's yield to maturity as it changes over the bond's life, plotted from its issue date to its maturity date.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A graph of a bond's price against its yield, showing the price-yield seesaw.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A graph of the relationship between term to maturity and yield, for comparable bonds, at a given point in time.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A graph of yield spreads between Treasury, municipal and corporate bonds by credit rating.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The yield curve is a cross-sectional snapshot: one date, many maturities, comparable bonds. A describes a time series of one bond, B is the price-yield curve used in pricing, and D describes sector yield spreads.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>According to the slides, which pair of statements about bond duration is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Higher coupon rates lengthen duration; higher yields to maturity lengthen duration.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Longer maturities shorten duration; higher coupon rates lengthen duration.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Duration always equals maturity for coupon bonds; it is shorter only for zero-coupon bonds.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Higher coupon rates shorten duration; higher yields to maturity shorten duration.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Higher coupons bring more present value forward, and higher yields shrink the weight of distant payments, so both shorten duration; longer maturity lengthens it. C has it backwards: a zero's duration equals its maturity, a coupon bond's is shorter.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which description matches the slides' <strong>tax swap</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Selling a low-yield bond and buying a comparable-quality bond with a higher yield, so that the portfolio's income rises without adding credit risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Selling a bond that has fallen in value, using the capital loss against other gains, and buying a comparable but not identical bond.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Buying equal amounts of bonds maturing in $3$, $5$, $7$ and $10$ years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Matching a portfolio's duration to the investor's horizon so that price and reinvestment effects roughly offset.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>That is the tax swap, with the wash-sale warning that the replacement cannot be the identical issue. A is a yield-pickup swap, C a ladder and D immunization. Actual tax treatment depends on the jurisdiction and current rules.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Two bonds have the same maturity and yield, but Bond A pays a $10\\%$ coupon and Bond B a $4\\%$ coupon. Why does A have the shorter Macaulay duration?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A larger share of A's present value arrives through coupons before maturity, so its present-value-weighted average time is earlier.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A's price is higher, and Macaulay duration is defined as the bond's price divided by its annual coupon, so a larger coupon gives a smaller ratio.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A's larger coupons are reinvested at a higher rate, which shortens its effective maturity and so its present-value-weighted average time.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Duration depends only on maturity, so the difference comes from rounding.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Duration weights each payment date by that payment's share of the price. Bigger coupons move weight forward. B invents a definition, C confuses reinvestment with timing (maturity does not change), and D ignores the weights.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Ignoring default, why can a zero-coupon bond held to maturity deliver exactly its quoted YTM, while a coupon bond held to maturity may not?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Zero-coupon bonds have no interest-rate risk at any time before maturity, because their price cannot move until the single payment at maturity falls due.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Coupon bonds always trade at a premium once coupons start, and amortizing that premium pulls their realized return below the quoted YTM at maturity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The zero has no interim coupons to reinvest; the coupon bond's realized return depends on the rates at which its coupons are reinvested.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The YTM of a zero-coupon bond is set by law.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>YTM's compound-return interpretation assumes coupons are reinvested at the YTM. A zero has no coupons, so only default (excluded here) can spoil the result. A is false: a zero's price swings a lot before maturity; it has the longest duration for its maturity.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Under strict <strong>market segmentation</strong>, what does an upward-sloping yield curve indicate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Investors expect short-term rates to rise, so long yields must exceed short yields to make holding either maturity equally attractive.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Investors see long-term bonds as riskier because of their greater price volatility, and demand a term premium to hold them to maturity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The issuer of long-term bonds is more likely to default.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The supply of short-term funds is high relative to borrowers' needs in that segment, so short yields are lower than long yields.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Segmentation explains each maturity's yield by supply and demand within its own segment. A is the expectations hypothesis and B is liquidity preference; the slides stress that segmentation does not read the slope as either of those.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A bond has $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par, a $7\\%$ annual coupon and $3$ years to maturity. The required yield is $9\\%$. What is its price?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}1{,}000.00$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}949.37$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}177.19$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}1{,}052.87$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$\\dfrac{70}{1.09} + \\dfrac{70}{1.09^2} + \\dfrac{1{,}070}{1.09^3} = 64.22 + 58.92 + 826.24 \\approx \\mathbf{\\text{RM}949.37}$. A discounts at the coupon rate; C forgets the par; D would be a premium price, impossible when the coupon is below the yield.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A $5$-year bond with $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par pays a $9\\%$ coupon semiannually. The market requires $8\\%$, quoted as a nominal annual yield compounded semiannually. What is its price?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}1{,}040.55$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}1{,}039.93$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}765.15$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}1{,}000.00$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Coupon $\\text{RM}45$, rate $4\\%$, $10$ periods: $45\\left[\\dfrac{1 - 1.04^{-10}}{0.04}\\right] + \\dfrac{1{,}000}{1.04^{10}} = 364.99 + 675.56 \\approx \\mathbf{\\text{RM}1{,}040.55}$. B is the annual-pay answer (close, but the wrong convention); C discounts at $8\\%$ per half-year; D would need the coupon to equal the yield.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A $3$-year bond pays a $10\\%$ annual coupon on $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par and is priced at par ($y = 10\\%$). Its Macaulay duration is about $2.7355$ years. What is the duration estimate of the percentage price change if the yield rises by $50$ basis points?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $-1.37\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $-13.68\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $-1.24\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $+1.24\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$D_{mod} = \\dfrac{2.7355}{1.10} = 2.4869$, and $\\dfrac{\\Delta P}{P} \\approx -2.4869 \\times 0.005 \\approx \\mathbf{-1.24\\%}$. A uses Macaulay instead of modified duration; B treats $50$ bp as $0.05$; D has the wrong sign. (Full repricing gives about $-1.23\\%$: the small gap is convexity.)</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Both yield $8\\%$: Bond Z is a $10$-year zero-coupon bond; Bond K is a $10$-year $8\\%$ annual-coupon bond (duration about $7.25$ years). Yields rise to $9\\%$. Which statement is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both fall by the same percentage because they have the same maturity and start from the same $8\\%$ yield to maturity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> K falls more because its coupons must now be reinvested, and each of its ten coupons is marked down at the higher $9\\%$ yield.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Neither price changes, because both will repay par at maturity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Z falls more (about $8.8\\%$ against $6.4\\%$) because all its value sits at year $10$, giving it the longer duration.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Price sensitivity follows duration, not maturity. Z's duration is $10$ years against K's $7.25$, so Z drops about $1 - (1.08 / 1.09)^{10} \\approx 8.8\\%$ while K drops about $6.4\\%$. Reinvestment (B) helps K over time; it does not make its price fall more. C confuses the eventual repayment with today's price.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A bond with $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par and $6$ years to maturity pays a $9\\%$ annual coupon and sells for $\\text{RM}1{,}080$. Which set of yields is consistent?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Coupon rate $9\\%$, current yield $9.72\\%$, YTM $10.4\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Coupon rate $9\\%$, current yield $8.33\\%$, YTM about $7.31\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Coupon rate $8.33\\%$, current yield $9\\%$, YTM $9\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Coupon rate $9\\%$, current yield $8.33\\%$, YTM about $9.6\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Current yield $= \\dfrac{90}{1{,}080} \\approx 8.33\\%$. A premium bond loses $\\text{RM}80$ on the way to par, so YTM is below the current yield: solving the price equation gives about $7.31\\%$. The premium ordering is coupon rate $&gt;$ CY $&gt;$ YTM. A and D put YTM on the wrong side; C swaps the definitions of coupon rate and current yield.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A $10$-year bond paying $\\text{RM}80$ a year trades at $\\text{RM}1{,}100$ (YTM about $6.6\\%$). It is callable in $3$ years at $\\text{RM}1{,}040$ (YTC about $5.55\\%$). Which analysis is strongest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The lower YTC is the more cautious planning figure: with the price above the call price and rates low, the issuer has an incentive to call, ending the high coupons early.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> YTM is always the right planning figure because a call is never certain, and the investor can simply hold the bond to its final maturity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> YTC must exceed YTM because the call price is above par, so an early call hands the investor a premium on top of the coupons already received and returns the money sooner.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The two yields cannot differ, because they share the same price.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A buyer paying above the call price loses the premium early if the bond is called, so the yield to the call scenario is lower. Neither yield is guaranteed, but the cautious investor plans on the less favourable plausible scenario. C fails here: the call price ($\\text{RM}1{,}040$) is below the purchase price, so an early call lowers the return.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>An investor says: \"The yield curve is inverted, so long-term bonds will certainly outperform short-term bonds over the next year.\" Which evaluation is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct, because inverted curves always predict falling rates, and falling rates raise long-bond prices by more than short-bond prices, since long bonds have the longer durations and so gain the most.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Correct only for corporate bonds, whose credit spreads narrow when the curve inverts and so add to the price gain from falling rates.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Overstated: an inverted curve is consistent with expected rate falls, but expectations can be wrong and premiums or segment supply and demand also shape it; if yields rise, long bonds lose more.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Incorrect, because an inverted curve proves that rates will rise back toward normal levels, so long bonds will lose more as their yields climb over the year.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The curve is evidence, not a certain forecast. Long bonds gain more if yields fall but, having longer durations, lose more if yields rise. D replaces one certainty claim with another.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>An investor must pay a fixed sum in exactly $3$ years. Bond X has a duration of $3.0$ years and Bond Y a duration of $6.5$ years; both are high quality with the same yield. Which choice and reason are best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Y, because its longer duration locks in a higher realized return whatever happens to rates, since its price gains always outweigh any shortfall in the reinvested coupons at year $3$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> X, because matching duration to the horizon lets price and reinvestment effects roughly offset for small parallel rate changes, provided it is rebalanced as duration drifts.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Y, because reinvestment risk disappears when duration is longer than the horizon, leaving only a price effect that averages out over the $3$ years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Either, because duration does not affect horizon wealth.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>With Y the investor must sell a long-duration bond at year $3$, so rising yields would leave a shortfall (price risk dominates). X is the immunizing choice, with the usual conditions: small parallel shifts, no default, periodic rebalancing.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A holder of an AA-rated, noncallable $5$-year bond yielding $5.0\\%$ is offered a swap into an A-rated $5$-year bond yielding $5.6\\%$ that is callable in $2$ years. Which evaluation is strongest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Accept: $60$ bp of extra yield is free return, since both bonds mature in $5$ years and an A rating is still investment grade, so the added default and call risks are negligible over such a short horizon.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Reject: swaps always generate tax losses and transaction costs that outweigh any yield pickup, whatever the two bonds' credit quality or call terms.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Accept: a callable bond is safer because the issuer may repay early, returning the investor's principal sooner and cutting exposure to later rate rises.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Investigate first: the pickup may only compensate for lower credit quality and the call option (which caps gains if rates fall), so compare credit, call terms, liquidity, taxes and costs.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A yield-pickup swap is worth doing only if the extra yield more than pays for the extra risks and costs. \"Comparable credit quality\" is part of the slide's definition, and here quality and call terms differ. C misreads the call: it benefits the issuer, not the holder.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>A fund must pay $\\text{RM}500{,}000$ in $4$ years. The flat yield is $6\\%$. It can buy Bond S (duration $2$ years) and Bond L (duration $6$ years). Which plan immunizes the liability, to a first approximation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Invest $\\text{RM}396{,}047$ (the liability's present value), half in S and half in L, so the portfolio duration is $4$ years; rebalance as time passes and yields change.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Invest $\\text{RM}500{,}000$ (the liability's face value), all in L, because longer bonds earn more and the extra return covers any change in rates.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Invest $\\text{RM}396{,}047$, all in S, because short bonds have no interest-rate risk, and roll the proceeds into new bonds as each one matures until the liability is due.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Invest $\\text{RM}396{,}047$, $25\\%$ in S and $75\\%$ in L, so the portfolio duration sits comfortably above the $4$-year horizon; rebalance as time passes and yields change.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>PV of the liability $= \\dfrac{500{,}000}{1.06^4} \\approx \\text{RM}396{,}047$. The weight on S solves $2w + 6(1 - w) = 4$, so $w = 0.5$. Both the value and the duration then match the liability. D gives $0.25(2) + 0.75(6) = 5$ years, too long; B and C ignore matching.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>A retiree wants cash every year for the next $5$ years and wants to avoid reinvesting all her money at one possibly bad moment. Which design best fits, using the chapter's strategies?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> One $20$-year callable bond with the highest available yield, selling part of the position each year to raise the cash she needs and keeping the rest invested to earn the higher coupon.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Sell all bonds and hold cash until rates rise, then buy a single $5$-year bond at the higher yield to fund the remaining years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A ladder: equal amounts of high-quality bonds maturing in each of years $1$ to $5$, each maturing rung spent or reinvested at the long end, with credit and liquidity reviewed yearly.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Lengthen duration as far as possible because rates might fall, locking in today's yields for longer and raising the bonds' prices if rates do fall.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The ladder matches the yearly cash need, spreads reinvestment dates and keeps liquidity. A concentrates call, credit and price risk; B gives up income and depends on a forecast; D is an active rate bet unrelated to her objective.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>A manager immunized a $6$-year liability last year with a portfolio of duration $6$. Which monitoring rule best keeps the immunization working?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Do nothing: once duration is matched, it stays matched until the liability is due, because the portfolio's duration and the remaining horizon both shrink by exactly one year each year, keeping the two in step without any trading.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Periodically re-match the portfolio's present value and duration to the remaining horizon, because duration does not fall one-for-one with time and also moves when yields change, allowing for transaction costs.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Double the portfolio's duration whenever rates rise, so the higher reinvestment income from the new rates arrives sooner and offsets the price loss.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Switch to the highest-yielding bonds each year to build a cushion of extra return that covers any shortfall as the duration drifts off the horizon.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>After a year the horizon is $5$ years but the portfolio's duration has typically fallen by less than a year, and yield changes move it too, so the match drifts. Rebalancing (net of costs) restores it. C and D are rate or yield bets that break the match.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A fund factsheet reports that the portfolio's duration was cut from $6$ years to $4$ years. If yields then rise by about $1$ percentage point across all maturities, what does the change roughly mean for the fund?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The price loss is roughly $4\\%$ instead of roughly $6\\%$ (treating these as modified durations), at the cost of smaller gains if yields fall.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The fund can no longer lose money from rate changes, because a $4$-year duration is shorter than the maturities of most of its bonds.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The fund's credit risk has fallen by a third, since shorter-duration portfolios hold bonds closer to repayment and so face less default risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The fund's income will rise by $2$ percentage points.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Duration scales the first-order price response: about $-D \\times \\Delta y$. Shortening duration reduces both the downside from rising yields and the upside from falling yields. It says nothing directly about credit risk (C) or income (D), and it does not remove rate risk (B).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A credit trader sees a corporate bond's yield rise by $35$ basis points after a downgrade. Its modified duration is $6.2$. What is the approximate price change?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $-21.7\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $+2.17\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $-0.22\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $-2.17\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>$\\Delta y = 0.0035$, so $\\dfrac{\\Delta P}{P} \\approx -6.2 \\times 0.0035 \\approx \\mathbf{-2.17\\%}$. A treats $35$ bp as $0.035$; C as $0.00035$; B has the wrong sign: yields up, price down.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>A bond has modified duration $7.0$ and convexity $60$. Using $\\dfrac{\\Delta P}{P} \\approx -D_{mod}\\Delta y + \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times \\text{convexity} \\times (\\Delta y)^2$, estimate the price change for a $1$ percentage-point rise in yield.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $-7.30\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $-7.00\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $-6.70\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $+6.70\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$-7.0 \\times 0.01 + 0.5 \\times 60 \\times 0.01^2 = -0.070 + 0.003 = \\mathbf{-6.70\\%}$. The convexity term is positive whichever way yields move, so it softens losses and adds to gains; B is the duration-only estimate the chapter warns is approximate, and A subtracts the convexity term.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>A semiannual-pay bond has a Macaulay duration of $4.5$ years and a YTM of $6\\%$, quoted as a nominal annual rate compounded semiannually. What is its modified duration?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $4.245$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $4.369$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $4.500$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $4.770$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>With $m = 2$, $D_{mod} = \\dfrac{D_{Mac}}{1 + y/m} = \\dfrac{4.5}{1.03} \\approx \\mathbf{4.369}$. A divides by $1.06$, the annual-pay formula applied to a semiannual bond; D multiplies instead of dividing.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>Yields fall sharply. A noncallable bond and an otherwise identical callable bond (callable now at $\\text{RM}1{,}050$) both start at $\\text{RM}1{,}000$. Which outcome is most likely?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The noncallable bond rises substantially; the callable bond's price stalls near $\\text{RM}1{,}050$, because investors expect the issuer to call and refinance at the lower rates.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both rise by the same amount, since they have the same coupon, maturity and issuer, and the call feature affects the price only once the issuer actually exercises it and redeems the bonds.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The callable bond rises more, because its call premium above par adds value for the holder that the otherwise identical noncallable bond does not offer.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both fall, because lower yields mean lower coupons.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The call option belongs to the issuer, who exercises it when rates fall. That caps the callable bond's price near the call price: the reason the slides recommend long <strong>noncallable</strong> bonds when rates are expected to fall, and why callable bonds offer higher yields.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>A perpetual bond pays a level coupon forever and is priced at a $5\\%$ yield. Using $D_{Mac} = \\dfrac{1+y}{y}$ for a level perpetuity, what is its Macaulay duration, and what does the answer show?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Infinite, because it never matures and duration averages the times of an endless stream of equal payments.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $5$ years, the yield expressed in years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $21$ years: although the maturity is infinite, distant payments carry little present value, so the weighted average time is finite.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $20$ years, which equals $\\dfrac{1}{y}$, the perpetuity's price-to-coupon ratio and so the average waiting time for its payments.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$\\dfrac{1.05}{0.05} = \\mathbf{21}$ years. Duration and maturity diverge completely here: present-value weighting makes far-off payments almost weightless. D is the price-to-coupon ratio, close but not the duration.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>The $1$-year rate is $4\\%$ and the $2$-year rate is $5\\%$ (annual compounding). Under the pure expectations hypothesis, what one-year rate do investors expect for year $2$?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $5.00\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $6.01\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $4.50\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $9.00\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$(1.05)^2 = (1.04)(1 + f)$, so $f = \\dfrac{1.1025}{1.04} - 1 \\approx \\mathbf{6.01\\%}$. The two-year yield is roughly the average of today's rate and the expected rate, so the expected rate must be well above $5\\%$. Under liquidity preference part of this forward rate is a term premium, so it overstates the expected rate.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>Two portfolios both have a duration of $7$ years: a \"bullet\" of $7$-year bonds, and a \"barbell\" of $1$-year and $20$-year bonds. Which evaluation is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> They are identical in every rate scenario because their durations match, and duration fully captures a portfolio's exposure to any shift in the yield curve.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The bullet is riskier in every scenario because all its bonds sit at one maturity, whereas the barbell's short and long ends offset each other whenever the curve twists, so its value barely changes.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The barbell has no reinvestment risk because part of it matures in one year and can be rolled over at whatever rates then prevail, locking in the target.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> For a small parallel shift they move about the same, but they differ under non-parallel shifts (a steepening hurts the barbell's long end more) and in convexity, so equal duration is not equal risk.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Duration is a first-order, parallel-shift measure. Portfolios with the same duration can respond differently to twists in the curve and to large moves. This is the chapter's warning that duration matching is a conditional approximation.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>An analyst values every bond with a single YTM. A colleague objects that each cash flow should be discounted at the spot (zero-coupon) rate for its own date. Which evaluation is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The colleague is right in principle: a YTM is a single average rate that depends on the cash flows' timing, so two bonds of the same maturity but different coupons can have different YTMs off the same spot curve.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The analyst is right: spot rates and YTM are always identical, because a coupon bond's YTM is simply the spot rate for its maturity date, so applying it to every cash flow gives exactly the same price as discounting each flow separately.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The colleague is wrong, because spot rates exist only for Treasury bills, so longer coupon bonds can be valued only with a single yield to maturity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It does not matter, because YTM ignores the time value of money, so any discounting method gives the same price for a given bond.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The notes flag the difference between a curve of coupon-bond yields and a zero-coupon spot curve. With a sloped spot curve, a high-coupon bond (more weight early) gets a YTM closer to the short spot rates. YTM is a convenient summary, not the rate for each date.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>In this chapter's horizon widget the bond's duration equals the horizon, and the realized return is lowest at the original yield, rising for any immediate parallel shift up or down. Evaluate: \"So duration matching guarantees at least the target return.\"</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> True in all circumstances, since the curve was drawn for every possible yield and the realized return never falls below its value at the original yield, whatever happens to rates afterwards or however often they change.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> False in all circumstances: duration matching never helps, because price and reinvestment effects move in the same direction when yields change.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> True only if the investor never receives coupons, because coupons reinvested after a fall in yields would leave the realized return below the target.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> True only under the model's assumptions (one immediate parallel shift of a flat curve, no default, no calls); non-parallel shifts, repeated unrebalanced rate changes, default or calls can cause a shortfall.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The convexity result is real but conditional. The slides' \"regardless of interest rates\" claim is an idealization; real curves twist, rates move many times and duration drifts, so immunization needs rebalancing and still carries residual risk.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>Which statement about the two duration measures is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Macaulay and modified duration are identical for every compounding convention, because both are present-value-weighted averages of the times to each of the bond's cash flows, so the compounding convention changes neither of the two measures.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Modified duration measures time in years and Macaulay duration measures price sensitivity, which is why Macaulay duration, not modified duration, is the one multiplied by the yield change in the first-order price-change approximation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Modified duration equals $-\\dfrac{1}{P}\\dfrac{dP}{dy}$ under the yield's own compounding convention; dividing Macaulay duration by $1 + y/m$ turns a time measure into this sensitivity, and under continuous compounding they coincide.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both measures stay constant over a bond's life, because they depend only on the coupon rate and the yield, not on the time remaining to maturity.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Differentiating the price with respect to the yield gives $-\\dfrac{D_{Mac}}{1 + y/m}$ per unit of price; as $m$ grows the divisor tends to $1$. B swaps the roles; D ignores that duration drifts with time and yields, which is why immunized portfolios need rebalancing.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>A researcher wants to test whether the slope of the yield curve really forecasts future short rates, as the pure expectations hypothesis implies. Which design is most informative?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Compare the term spread observed at each date with the short-rate changes that actually followed, over many dates and rate cycles, and check whether the slope predicts them one-for-one or a time-varying term premium remains.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Look at today's yield curve, report its slope, and read an upward slope as proof that short rates will rise by the amount the term spread implies.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Survey investors once and ask whether they expect rates to rise, then compare the share expecting a rise with the current slope of the yield curve.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Compare corporate and government bond yields on a single day across all maturities, and attribute any difference between the two curves' slopes to expected changes in future short rates rather than to credit or liquidity premiums.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A single curve (B), one survey (C) or one day's credit spread (D) cannot separate expectations from term premiums. Relating past slopes to the rates that actually followed, across many periods, is the standard way to test whether the curve's shape is a forecast or contains a premium, exactly the distinction this chapter asks you to keep.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
